==============================================================
Full-system binary-only instrumentation in Renode for afl-fuzz
==============================================================

  (See ../README.md for the general instruction manual.)

0) TL;DR: just run anything
---------------------------

1. Download latest portable build of Renode from https://builds.renode.io/
   and unpack it to ~/renode or anywhere else.

2. Build some system to test, e.g. Zephyr shell sample for nRF52840 and
   copy it to this renode_mode directory (you will need Zephyr SDK, see
   their docs[0]):

   $ west build -b nrf52840dk_nrf52840 samples/subsys/shell/shell_module
   $ cp build/zephyr/zephyr.elf /path/to/AFL/renode_mode

[0]: https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/develop/getting_started/

3. (If needed, edit the example.resc file; by default nRF52840 is used)
4. (If needed, edit the afl_uart.py file; serial device might be named
    differently on other boards/targets)
5. (If needed, adjust any other constants in afl_renode.py)
6. Run:

   $ cd /path/to/AFL
   $ make
   $ mkdir INPUTS
   $ echo 'abcdefxyz' > INPUTS/some-testcase  # true corpus works better
   $ PATH=$PATH:<path-to-renode> AFL_SKIP_BIN_CHECK=1 ./afl-fuzz -t 15000 -i INPUTS -o OUTPUTS -R -- renode_mode/example.resc

Notes:

    On Cortex-M, jumping to a NULL function pointer raises a UsageFault
    instead of executing at address 0, so besides PC 0 the harness also
    treats entering ``k_sys_fatal_error_handler`` (Zephyr) as a crash.
    Override the crash symbols with a comma-separated list in
    ``AFL_RENODE_CRASH_SYMBOLS``.

    If ``core_pattern`` pipes to a crash handler (e.g. systemd-coredump)
    and you cannot change it, set ``AFL_I_DONT_CARE_ABOUT_MISSING_CRASHES=1``;
    crashes are reported by the harness, not by real core dumps.

    The ``efr32mg_sltb004a`` Zephyr board is now named ``sltb004a``.

    While ``renode_mode/example.resc`` is executable, it shouldn't be run as a separate, standalone Renode script - it depends on several settings provided by ``afl-renode-trace``.

1) Introduction
---------------

Renode[1] is an open-source simulation framework developed by Antmicro,
allowing users to run their unmodified software on a range of embedded
targets, ranging from MCUs to multi-core Linux-capable systems, on
various architectures, like ARM Cortex-A,R,M, RISC-V, POWER and others.
Renode allows for single- and multi-node simulation via various
connectivity methods: Ethernet, BLE, IEEE802.15.4 etc.

[1]: https://www.renode.io

The code in this directory is used for a standalone feature that
leverages Renode and allows callers to obtain instrumentation output for
black-box, closed-source binaries for full bare-metal systems rather
than user-mode executables.  This mechanism can be then used by afl-fuzz
to stress-test targets in a context of a full system simulation,
including the OS and HAL level, or targets that couldn't be built as
user-mode binaries.

The idea is heavily based on deferred fork server mode and QEMU mode.

2) How to use
-------------

The feature is implemented with a fairly small plugin for Renode.  As
usual with Renode, you need a Renode script and platform description.
See its documentation[2] for more details about that.  The default
harness assumes a single-node setup instrumenting code executed on
sysbus.cpu and feeding input through sysbus.uart0 char-by-char whenever
the CPU looks idle.

[2]: https://renode.readthedocs.io/

The example script ready for fuzzing is in the example.resc file.  It is
based on the nRF52840 platform, but can be easily adapted to other
Renode-supported targets.

If you want to change the harness, you can do so by copying the
afl_uart.py file over, changing it, and importing the new file instead
in your .resc file of choice, 'import antigravity'-style.  See the
example.resc file for how to do it.

3) Zephyr syscall harness
-------------------------

afl_syscall.py and example-syscall.resc fuzz Zephyr system calls from
user mode threads on cortex_r8_virtual.  The guest application is in
zephyr-syscall-fuzz/; it is built for one syscall group at a time (see
"Syscall groups" below), e.g. for k_poll and friends:

  west build -b cortex_r8_virtual -d build-g-poll \
      renode_mode/zephyr-syscall-fuzz -- -DEXTRA_CONF_FILE=groups/poll.conf

Each test case is written into the guest's afl_input buffer right after
a machine reset (afl_renode.always_reset; the ELF is loaded once and its
LOAD segments are written back after each reset, see "Renode memory"
below), and a run ends when the guest
calls afl_done (success), afl_bug (kernel fault/panic/assert, reported
as SIGABRT) or afl_hang, or when the host watchdog expires after
AFL_RENODE_MAX_QUANTA quanta or AFL_RENODE_MAX_WALL seconds of wall
clock (default 10, keep it below afl-fuzz -t; see "Instance deaths"
below; 0 disables it).  User thread oopses are expected and are
not crashes.  An optional second argument to afl-renode-trace selects
the ELF:

  AFL_KILL_SIGNAL=18 AFL_FORK_SERVER_KILL_SIGNAL=15 \
  afl-fuzz -t 20000 -i renode_mode/zephyr-syscall-fuzz/seeds/poll -o out \
      -R -- renode_mode/example-syscall.resc zephyr-syscall-fuzz.elf

Building with CONFIG_SYSCALL_FUZZ_SELFTEST=y plants kernel bugs (input
bytes 0xF0/0xF1/0xF2: panic, fault, assertion) to check that crash
detection works.

The guest build has no console output, for speed. Before reporting a
failure the guest fills an afl_crash record (fatal reason, thread,
exception frame, DFSR/DFAR/IFSR/IFAR, assertion file:line, and the input
offset of the op being executed). With AFL_RENODE_CRASH_INFO=<dir> the
harness decodes it into <dir>/<sha1 of input>.txt, so each file in
crashes/ can be matched with `sha1sum`. To look at a single input:

  renode_mode/zephyr-syscall-fuzz/replay.sh zephyr-syscall-fuzz.elf <input>

prints the same summary, and with `-g 3333` keeps the crashed machine
alive behind a GDB server (`-w` waits for the debugger before booting).
Replay on the same ELF that found the crash: inputs may contain raw
addresses that are only meaningful for that build.

Coverage is selected with AFL_RENODE_COVERAGE:

  window  (default) AFL-style edge coverage, only between user_entry and
          the afl_* marker that ends the run; boot and setup are the same
          for every input and would only add noise
  kernel  like window, but without the harness's own functions, listed
          in <ELF>.cov (written at build time by cov_ranges.py)
  all     the original afl_renode block coverage, from reset

In 5 minute side-by-side runs, window reached 2745 exact kernel edges,
all 1521 and kernel 1216: the harness's own coverage tells AFL which op
sequences are new, and is a useful stepping stone. To compare corpora on
exact kernel edges, replay them through one Renode instance:

  AFL_RENODE_COVERAGE=kernel renode_mode/zephyr-syscall-fuzz/replay_corpus.py \
      -e edges.txt renode_mode/example-syscall.resc zephyr-syscall-fuzz.elf \
      out/default/queue/id*
  wc -l edges.txt

Do not switch the block hook on and off from cpu.AddHook callbacks: the
change takes effect at nondeterministic points, which dropped stability
to ~77% and produced false hangs. afl_syscall keeps the hook installed
and gates coverage in Python instead, at no measurable cost.

Syscall groups
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

One group per build (groups/<name>.conf), each with its own seeds/
directory. Groups favour syscalls that are rarely used by in-tree code
and by external Zephyr applications, as heavily used ones are already
exercised by tests and products.

  poll          k_poll, k_poll_signal_* over sem/fifo/msgq/pipe/signal
  objects       k_object_alloc{,_size}/release, k_object_access_grant/
                check, *_init/alloc_init of every kernel object type,
                k_event_*, k_condvar_*, k_thread_stack_alloc/free,
                k_thread_create/join/abort from user mode
  futex         k_futex_wait/wake, sys_mutex (futex implementation),
                k_msgq_put_front/peek_at/purge/get_attrs,
                k_queue_insert/cancel_wait
  futex-kmutex  same input format, sys_mutex on top of k_mutex
                (build with groups/futex-kmutex.conf, seeds/futex)
  thread        k_thread_name_set/copy, priority/deadline, suspend/
                resume/wakeup/join/abort, timeout getters,
                k_thread_stack_space_get, custom data

The input is a byte stream of ops: an opcode byte, then the op's
arguments read in the order the group code consumes them (u32s are
little endian).  Arguments come from small pools in main.c that mix
valid and hostile values: fz_ptr() (scratch buffer, NULL, kernel data,
raw u32, end of scratch, end of the user partition, code), fz_len()
(small, raw, near SIZE_MAX), fz_timeout() (K_NO_WAIT, a few ticks,
K_FOREVER, raw) and fz_thread() (self, the other worker, dormant, dead,
ungranted main thread, NULL, raw).  CONFIG_SYSCALL_FUZZ_WORKERS user
threads share the stream: when one blocks the next one continues
reading it, so a "wait" op followed by a "wake" op does what it says.
gen_seeds.py writes the seeds for objects/futex/thread.

campaign.sh starts one parallel campaign (one -M, JOBS-1 -S) per group,
copying the ELF, .cov and .config into the output directory so crashes
stay replayable after rebuilds:

  B=../build-g
  renode_mode/zephyr-syscall-fuzz/campaign.sh -d 172800 -j 4 \
      objects=$B-objects/zephyr/zephyr.elf futex=$B-futex/zephyr/zephyr.elf \
      futex-kmutex=$B-futex-kmutex/zephyr/zephyr.elf \
      thread=$B-thread/zephyr/zephyr.elf

-n prints the commands without running them.  Each instance is one
afl-fuzz plus one Renode process (~1 core, ~350-450 MB RSS).  Instances
run detached (setsid) in the background and campaign.sh returns at once
(-w waits and prints the status at the end); OUT/<group>/<name>.pid and
.log are kept per instance, and OUT/campaign.conf holds the deadline.
Other modes act on a running campaign:

  campaign.sh -S OUT               status of every instance
  campaign.sh -R OUT GROUP/NAME..  restart dead instances, resuming their
                                   queue (-i -) for the time left
  campaign.sh -K OUT               stop the supervisor, then all instances
  campaign.sh -P OUT               (re)start the supervisor

A supervisor (watchdog.py --supervise) is started detached with the
campaign and runs until its deadline, so the campaign survives without
anyone watching: it restarts instances whose afl-fuzz died (with -R, at
most --max-restarts per instance per hour), and SIGKILLs a Renode whose
heartbeat file (OUT/<group>/<name>.alive, touched by the harness at
least every 10 s while runs complete) is older than --stall seconds, so
that instance is then restarted too.  Its actions go to
OUT/actions.log (and OUT/supervisor.log, with log tails).

watchdog.py OUT monitors a campaign: every --interval seconds it samples
the instances (fuzzer_stats, Renode RSS, disk space) into
OUT/metrics.csv, and it exits when something needs attention: 0 when the
campaign is over, 3 on events (an instance died, Renode RSS above
--rss-mb or grown by --rss-growth-mb, exec/s below --min-eps, stats
stalled, new crashes/hangs, low disk, a supervisor action, or no
supervisor running), 4 for a periodic report
(--report-hours).  While the supervisor runs, dead and stalled
instances are reported through its actions instead.  New crashes are grouped by signature (assertion, or
fault reason and PC from crash-info/) with an example input.  Each event
is reported once (OUT/watchdog.ack), so it can simply be run again after
handling it; touch OUT/<group>/<name>.retired to stop watching an
instance.  The intended loop, e.g. from an agent session: start the
campaign detached, run watchdog.py in the foreground, on exit triage
(replay.sh on the group's copied ELF), restart with -R if needed, and
run watchdog.py again.  The supervisor keeps the campaign alive if that
loop is interrupted.

Disk usage: the output defaults to $HOME/zfuzz/<date> (or
$ZFUZZ_CAMPAIGNS/<date>), and campaign.sh warns if OUT is under /tmp.
Instances run with TMPDIR=OUT/tmp, because Renode and dotnet put their
scratch files there (~1 MB per Renode instance, not always removed),
along with a crash dump each time Renode is killed, and debug pipes and
sockets.  So nothing grows in /tmp, and `rm -rf OUT/tmp` cleans up after
a campaign.  The socket path limit means OUT/tmp may be at most 60
characters long.  The AFL queue dominates: about 4 KB per corpus entry,
roughly 3-4 MB per instance after 10 minutes, so plan for a few GB for a
weekend with 16 instances.  Builds are only read at launch (the ELF is
copied), so they can stay on a small /tmp.  replay.sh and
replay_corpus.py use TMPDIR too.

Challenges and how they were handled
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

* Blocking syscalls.  k_poll only needs one thread, but futex/condvar/
  event/join waits block forever.  Two user workers share the input,
  the supervisor (higher priority, kernel mode) ends the run when all
  workers are blocked or dead, the system is idle for IDLE_TICKS, or
  HANG_MS of uptime elapsed.  Real hangs are therefore only reported
  when the kernel itself stops making progress.

* Object handles.  Syscalls take kernel object pointers that must be in
  the kernel object table and granted.  Inputs mostly select from per-group handle tables (static
  granted objects, objects not granted to the caller, objects of the
  wrong type, dynamic slots filled by k_object_alloc, NULL), with a raw
  u32 pointer as just one of the choices, so most inputs get past the
  verifier while the hostile cases are still covered.

* Thread targets.  Thread syscalls are interesting on threads in odd
  states, so fz_thread() offers a dormant (K_FOREVER-delayed) thread, an
  already dead one, the ungranted main thread, besides self/peer.

* Determinism / harness self-corruption.  First small runs showed
  hangs that did not replay: syscalls handed a raw pointer into the user
  partition overwrote the stream position/length, changing what the
  rest of the input did.  All stream state now lives in struct fz_mem
  at the start of the partition (copies off the end of scratch cannot
  reach it), the kernel keeps its own copy of the length, and the
  supervisor ends the run deterministically if the state was tampered.
  Stability is 100% in all groups.

* Dynamic thread stacks.  k_thread_stack_alloc() from the static pool
  (the default) returns stacks the calling user thread has no
  permission on, so k_thread_create() always failed with "bad stack
  object".  groups/objects.conf sets DYNAMIC_THREAD_PREFER_ALLOC so
  stacks come from the (granted) heap first.

* sys_mutex only enters the kernel when contended (FUTEX
  implementation): uncontended lock/unlock is an atomic CAS in user
  space.  The futex seeds lock from one worker and then contend from the
  other, so the kernel side is reached.

* Separate heap.  K_HEAP fz_heap (SYSCALL_FUZZ_HEAP_SIZE) is the user
  threads' resource pool, so out-of-memory paths are deterministic.

* Renode memory.  The first test campaign showed every Renode growing by
  ~200 MB/min (85 KB per test case), i.e. out of memory within hours.
  The cause was the resc "reset" macro reloading the ELF on every
  Machine.Reset(): LoadELF leaks ~175 KB of managed memory per call in
  Renode 1.17 (reset alone: a few KB, levelling off).  The ELF is now
  loaded once; afl_syscall.py snapshots its LOAD segments (with the
  zeroed .bss) and the entry PC before the first run and writes them
  back after each reset.  Renode now stays at ~350-450 MB and exec/s
  went up ~20%.  Machine.Reset() also drops the symbols LoadELF added,
  and reloading them (LoadSymbolsFrom) leaks too, ~140 KB per call: the
  first weekend campaign did it on every failure, and the thread group
  (~4700 hangs per instance) grew to 2.2 GB in 62 h, a third of it
  explained by this.  The harness now keeps the addresses it needs from
  before the first reset and symbolizes crash records from the ELF's
  own symbol table (afl_syscall.elf_symbols), never asking Renode; 2000
  resets plus describe_failure() now grow RSS no more than resets alone
  (~50 MB, levelling off).  The rest of the growth (~20 MB/h) is not
  explained yet; watchdog.py watches RSS (--rss-growth-mb).

* Instance deaths.  In the first weekend campaign (12 instances, left
  unmonitored by mistake) 9 instances died within 1-22 h, all with
  "Unable to communicate with fork server".  Renode is both the fork
  server and the target and reports its own pid as the child, so when a
  run exceeds afl-fuzz -t, afl-fuzz SIGKILLs Renode itself and aborts
  (reproduced with -t 3000 on hang inputs).  The harness's hang limit
  was in quanta (simulated time), whose wall-clock cost grows with host
  load, so under a loaded host some runs passed 15 s.  Three fixes:
  campaign.sh sets AFL_KILL_SIGNAL=18 (SIGCONT, a no-op) so a timeout no
  longer kills Renode (AFL_FORK_SERVER_KILL_SIGNAL=15 keeps shutdown
  working); the harness also ends a run after AFL_RENODE_MAX_WALL
  seconds (10, below -t 20000), so it always reports first; and the
  supervisor restarts dead instances and kills a Renode that is truly
  wedged (with SIGCONT, afl-fuzz would otherwise wait for it forever).
  Wall-clock hangs are marked "hang: ... wall clock" in crash-info, as
  they may not replay on an idle host.

* Coverage is verified, not assumed: replay_corpus.py -e dumps the
  edges of the seeds; each seed was checked to reach the kernel
  function it targets.

Known bugs found (reproducers in zephyr-syscall-fuzz/known-bugs/, run
them with replay.sh on a build with CONFIG_SYSCALL_FUZZ_SKIP_KNOWN_BUGS=n):

* k_thread_create() from user mode with a very small stack_size: the
  verifier only checks that the size fits the stack object, but
  reserve_stack_headroom() (TLS, userspace local data) and the initial
  exception frame are not checked against buf_size, so the kernel
  writes below the stack object.  With heap allocated stacks this
  overwrites the previous heap chunk header and later faults in
  sys_heap_free() (K_ERR_ARM_BACKGROUND_FAULT) or similar; the new
  thread itself takes a permission fault on its TLS.
  (thread-create-stack-size-0, thread-create-stack-size-16)

* k_msgq_alloc_init() accepts msg_size 0 (z_thread_malloc(0) succeeds),
  and the first k_msgq_put() then trips the write_ptr assertion in
  put_msg_in_queue() (msg_q.c).  Kernel panic from user mode on
  CONFIG_ASSERT=y builds. (msgq-alloc-init-msg-size-0)

* Releasing a dynamic k_msgq or k_stack that was never initialized:
  k_object_alloc() does not zero the object, and when the last reference
  goes away (k_object_release() or thread exit) unref_check() calls
  z_msgq_cleanup()/z_stack_cleanup() regardless, which reads stale heap
  contents as flags/buffer_start and k_free()s them (double free panic
  with SYS_HEAP_HARDENING, otherwise heap corruption).  K_OBJ_TIMER
  already guards on K_OBJ_FLAG_INITIALIZED; msgq and stack do not.
  (msgq-release-uninitialized)

* Two user threads deadlocking on k_mutex trip the MUTEX_DEADLOCK_DETECT
  assertion (mutex.c, default y with CONFIG_ASSERT), i.e. a user thread
  can panic the kernel on assert-enabled builds; arguably by design, but
  it should probably return an error to user mode instead.
  (kmutex-deadlock-assert, futex-kmutex build)

* Dynamically allocated user stacks look too big to the verifier:
  for k_thread_stack_alloc(K_USER) / k_object_alloc_size(
  K_OBJ_THREAD_STACK_ELEMENT), dynamic_object_create() records the whole
  allocation (z_stack_data, privileged stack, alignment padding and the
  stack) as the object size, so z_vrfy_k_thread_create() accepts a
  stack_size well beyond what was requested (e.g. 1536 on a 512 byte
  stack) and the user stack region then covers the thread's own
  privileged stack.  On cortex_r8_virtual the oversized region is also
  not aligned to its (power of two) size, and the new thread takes a
  permission fault on its first instruction (K_ERR_ARM_PERMISSION_FAULT
  in z_thread_entry); with an MPU without that constraint it would give
  user mode write access to the privileged stack.
  (thread-create-dyn-stack-oversize)

CONFIG_SYSCALL_FUZZ_SKIP_KNOWN_BUGS (default y) avoids them so long
campaigns are not filled with variants: too small stacks and msg_size 0
are not passed, threads are not created on dynamic stacks with a size
above the one allocated, dynamic msgq/stack objects are initialized
right after allocation (the INIT op then fails on them), and
MUTEX_DEADLOCK_DETECT defaults to n.  The stream format is the same either way.
